The plugin is a Hermes platform adapter: a Python class that reads a bot key, pulls
`GET /api/bot/v1/session`, `/rooms/messages`, `/tasks` and `/assistance/tasks`
from the Bunny Space relay, signs the calls, wraps third-party content in the
boundary the relay declares, and posts answers back with `/rooms/reply` and
`/update`. What was rewritten is that wire and those rules, in Common Lisp against
Nodecode's cell contract: the section and its refusal, one worker lap, the HMAC
signature, the boundary and its neutralized marks, the 45-second heartbeat and the
exponential backoff are the original's; the seams are Nodecode's, so a room or a
task is a session (`bunny-room-<id>`, `bunny-task-<id>`), a turn is `nle:submit`,
and an answer is the session's own (`nle`/`nlk` rather than a `BasePlatformAdapter`).
Left out: the private assistance worker (`ISOLATED_ASSISTANCE_V1`), whose gate asks
for a Hermes-only per-platform toolset list and a call-isolation proof that has no
counterpart here, so no assistance task is claimed, leased or renewed; the WebSocket
wake stream and its 25-second keepalive, since the poll lap alone covers the
cadence; the media download (an image a task carries is announced, not fetched); the
`BUNNY_SPACE_ALLOW_ALL_USERS` kill switch, whose job the section's `allow_all`
member does here; and Hermes's own `/sethome` and environment-variable seeding,
which the `channels.bunny_space` section replaces.
